The secret is to keep the file and ndb info for active connections that are already in the store.  For comparison, this is what we do downstream:
```[js]
        let proxyDevices = devices
          .filter((d) => getters['isProxyDevice'](d.data))
          .map((device) => ({
            ...device.data,
            WebSocketEndpoint: device.data?.Oem?.OpenBMC?.WebSocketEndpoint ?? defaultDevice.WebSocketEndpoint,
            file: null,
          }));
        // if there are no proxy devices, add the default device
        if (proxyDevices.length === 0) {
          proxyDevices = [defaultDevice];
        }
        // Don't kill current connections on a reload of data
        // override items in the proxyDevices array with current active devices
        // Keep the current file and nbd objects
        proxyDevices.forEach((device) => {
          const currentDevice = state.proxyDevices.find((d) => d.Id === device.Id);
          if (currentDevice) {
            Object.assign(device, {
              file: currentDevice?.file,
              nbd: currentDevice?.nbd,
            });
          }
        });
```

Implemented connection preservation in setProxyDevicesData 
mutation. When data reloads, active connections are maintained:

if (currentDevice?.isActive && currentDevice?.file) {
  return {
    ...newDevice,
    file: currentDevice.file,      // Preserved
    nbd: currentDevice.nbd,        // Preserved
    isActive: currentDevice.isActive,
  };
}

This prevents WebSocket connection loss during SPA navigation, exactly 
